  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 934 PM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nELEVATED FLASH FLOODING THREAT THROUGH MONDAY EVENING:\n\nA significant plume of moisture remains in place through at least \nMonday evening for much of the area. PWATs continue to hold steady \nat 160-180% of normal, with localized pockets of 200% of normal. \nThis will mean another day of scattered to widespread showers and \nstorms. Primary threats continue to be localized heavy rainfall, \nlightning, and gusty outflow winds (35-45 mph). This inherently \nmeans there is also a risk for flash flooding and debris flows along \nrecent burn scars, susceptible terrain, and areas of\nincreasingly saturated soils. As the richer moisture hovers \naround the Four Corners area, the flash flooding concern favors \nareas along and south of I-70 Monday. This will be especially \ntrue of areas that have received multiple rounds of \nprecipitation over the past few days. For those reasons, a Flood\nWatch remains in effect for much of eastern Utah and western \nColorado through Monday evening. See the hydrology section below\nfor more information on that. \n\nTUESDAY + BEYOND: \n\nA drier airmass moves into the area by Tuesday and remains in place \nfor much of the work week. Although afternoon showers and storms \nremain in the forecast over the higher terrain, but coverage will be \nmuch more isolated than previous days. A return to seasonable \ntemperatures is also in the forecast for the first half of the week. \n\nModels continue to be in agreement that another push of monsoonal \nmoisture is on the horizon for the Intermountain West this coming \nweekend. But, that period still remains far out so the details \nare vague yet. Continue to monitor the forecast as changes are \nmade.",
